<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Emirate of Tagant</h1><p class="st-meta">1760 CE – 1905 CE (approx.) · capital Tijikja</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The emirate of the Idaw Aish in central Mauritania, ruled from Tijikja, where French colonial expansion met fierce resistance in 1905.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Idaw Aish, a group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> origin that adopted the warrior way of life of the Hassani, rose to dominance in the Tagant plateau of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> in the 18th century. By around 1760 their chiefs ruled as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tijikja">Tijikja</a> as a main centre.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> ruled over warrior lineages, scholarly tribes, tributaries and enslaved oasis cultivators, taxing date palms, herds and caravans. Tagant was frequently torn by feuds between rival branches of the ruling family and wars with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-brakna">Emirate of Brakna</a> and other neighbours. Emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakar-ould-soueid-ahmed">Bakkar ould Swayd Ahmad</a>, whose reign spanned much of the 19th century, was its most famous ruler.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xavier-coppolani">Xavier Coppolani</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tijikja">Tijikja</a> in 1905 and was assassinated there the same year. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakar-ould-soueid-ahmed">Bakkar</a>, then very old, was killed fighting the French in 1905, and Tagant was absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-mauritania">French Mauritania</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/emirate-of-tagant.jpg" alt="Emirate of Tagant" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: bob rayner · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Passe_de_djouk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1760 CE – 1905 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tijikja</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bakkar ould Swayd Ahmad</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (Maliki)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hassaniya Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Mauritania</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tagant, Idaw Aish</dd></dl></aside></div>

